1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 g5 {The Brentano Defence.} 4. d4 Nxd4 5. Nxd4 exd4
6. Qxd4 Qf6 7. Be3 ({A new move. Dr. Euwe in his Theorie der Schaakopeningen,
no.10, The Hague, 1948, p.109, suggests either Trebitsch's move} 7. Qe3 {
to which Brentano used to reply with the paradoxical key-move of the defence,
namely} Bd6 $1) ({or, alternatively, Dr. Euwe recommends} 7. Qxf6 Nxf6 8. e5
Ng4 ({but this line too is not entirely convincing, partly because Black seems
to be able to play instead} 8... Nd5 9. Bxg5 Bg7 10. O-O O-O) 9. Bxg5 {, etc,
a variation in which White is supposed to win a pawn on move 23}) {Horne is
developing all his pieces in the normal way. Black must be on his guard.} 7...
Bg7 8. Qxf6 Bxf6 9. Nc3 Ne7 10. O-O-O a6 11. Bc4 d6 12. e5 Bxe5 13. Bxg5 Be6
14. Bxe6 fxe6 15. f4 Bxc3 16. bxc3 Rf8 17. Rd3 Kd7 18. g4 Rf7 19. Re1 Ng6 20.
Rde3 Nxf4 21. Bxf4 Rxf4 22. Rxe6 Rf7 23. g5 Rg8 24. h4 Rgg7 {In my opinion
Black has equalised. Later on in this game (move 40) White overplayed his hand
and lost an exciting rook ending, as follows:} 25. Kd2 Kc6 26. Kd3 Rf4 27. R1e4
Rf3+ 28. Re3 Rf4 29. R6e4 Rff7 30. Kd4 b5 31. Re6 Rf5 32. R3e4 Rgf7 33. Re7 Rf4
34. Rxf7 Rxf7 35. Rg4 Kd7 36. h5 c5+ 37. Ke3 Ke6 38. Re4+ Kd5 39. Rf4 Rg7 40.
Rf5+ Kc4 41. Kf4 Kxc3 42. Rf6 d5 43. Rxa6 b4 44. Ra5 c4 45. Rxd5 Kxc2 46. Kf5
Rf7+ 47. Ke6 Ra7 48. Rb5 Kc3 49. g6 hxg6 50. hxg6 Rxa2 51. Rg5 Ra6+ 52. Ke5
Ra5+ 53. Kf4 Ra8 {Source: CHESS, March 1949, Vol.14/162, p152} 0-1

